window.docContents[80].resolve({"/Elementary-Functions/___4___35-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___35-Identities--___4___35-Identities":{"contents":"DLMF §4.35 gives the addition, product, multiple-angle, and modulus identities for\nthe complex hyperbolic functions. Each display below links directly to its\nformula-specific DLMF entry. Quotient identities include the nonvanishing\nhypotheses needed for ordinary complex division.\n\nThe local complexCoth definition is a totalized quotient: at a zero of\nComplex.sinh, Lean's division returns zero rather than an analytic pole.\nAccordingly, the coth formulas below explicitly assume the relevant sinh\nvalues are nonzero; outside that domain the definition is only a totalized\nconvenience, not a claim that coth is analytic there.\n\n\n\nAddition of sinh arguments expands both the sum and difference with their signed cross terms.\n\nAddition of cosh arguments gives the corresponding even-function sum and difference forms.\n\nThe tanh addition and subtraction quotients require nonzero cosh factors and displayed denominators.\n\nThe coth addition and subtraction quotients are stated on the pole-free domain of the reciprocal vocabulary.\n\nThe sum of two sinh values is the product of sinh and cosh at the half-sum and half-difference.\n\nThe difference of two sinh values is the complementary cosh–sinh product at half arguments.\n\nThe sum of two cosh values factors into cosh at the half-sum and half-difference.\n\nThe difference of two cosh values factors into the corresponding sinh product.\n\nThe tanh sum and difference are written as sinh quotients under the nonzero cosh hypotheses.\n\nThe coth sum and difference are written as sinh quotients under the nonzero sinh hypotheses.\n\nThe fundamental hyperbolic Pythagorean identity relates the squared cosh and sinh values.\n\nTaking the reciprocal square of cosh yields the stated identity involving tanh.\n\nTaking the reciprocal square of sinh yields the corresponding identity involving coth.\n\nThe product of two sinh values is recovered by subtracting the two cosh addition forms.\n\nThe product of two cosh values is recovered by adding the two cosh addition forms.\n\nThe mixed sinh–cosh product is recovered by adding the two sinh addition forms.\n\nThe difference of squared sinh values factors into sinh at the sum and difference arguments.\n\nThe difference of squared cosh values has the same sum-and-difference factorization.\n\nThe mixed sum of squared sinh and cosh values factors into a product of cosh terms.\n\nFor nonnegative real x, the principal square-root branch gives the half-angle sinh identity.\n\nFor nonnegative real x, the principal square-root branch gives the half-angle cosh identity.\n\nFor positive real x, the principal half-angle square-root quotient agrees with both rational forms.\n\nSinh is odd, giving the sign reversal under negation.\n\nCosh is even, so negating the argument leaves it unchanged.\n\nTanh is odd, with the same totalized complex quotient convention as Mathlib.\n\nThe double-angle sinh identity includes its tanh quotient form under the displayed denominator condition.\n\nThe three equivalent double-angle cosh forms are valid throughout the complex domain.\n\nThe double-angle tanh quotient requires the displayed nonzero denominator.\n\nThe triple-angle sinh polynomial is expressed in powers of sinh.\n\nThe triple-angle cosh polynomial is expressed in powers of cosh.\n\nThe quadruple-angle sinh expansion separates the two cubic mixed terms.\n\nThe quadruple-angle cosh expansion is the symmetric fourth-degree expression.\n\nThe integer-multiple formula identifies powers of cosh z plus or minus sinh z.\n\nThe sinh value on a complex argument is decomposed into its real and imaginary coordinates.\n\nThe cosh value on a complex argument is decomposed into its real and imaginary coordinates.\n\nThe tanh addition quotient at a doubled argument requires a nonzero cosh denominator.\n\nThe coth subtraction quotient at a doubled argument requires a nonzero sinh denominator.\n\nThe modulus of sinh is the square root of the sum of the squared real-coordinate terms.\n\nThe modulus of cosh is the square root of the sum of the squared real-coordinate terms.\n\nThe modulus of tanh is the square root of the quotient of the corresponding double-angle quantities, under the nonzero cosh condition.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.35 Identities","header":"§4.35 Identities","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___35-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___35-Identities--___4___35-Identities"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___41-Sums/#chapter-4-section-41":{"contents":"Source: NIST Digital Library of Mathematical Functions, §4.41.\n\n\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions","header":"4.41. §4.41 Sums","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___41-Sums/#chapter-4-section-41"},"/Elementary-Functions/___4___8-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___8-Identities--___4___8-Identities--___4___8_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms":{"contents":"\\mathrm{Ln}(z_1z_2)=\\mathrm{Ln}z_1+\\mathrm{Ln}z_2.\nFor nonzero real arguments this is the real logarithm product law.\n\n\\ln(z_1z_2)=\\ln z_1+\\ln z_2,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\operatorname{ph}z_1+\\operatorname{ph}z_2\\leq\\pi.\nPositive real arguments have zero phase, so the principal condition is automatic.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(z_1/z_2)=\\mathrm{Ln}z_1-\\mathrm{Ln}z_2.\nFor nonzero real arguments this is the real logarithm quotient law.\n\n\\ln(z_1/z_2)=\\ln z_1-\\ln z_2,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\operatorname{ph}z_1-\\operatorname{ph}z_2\\leq\\pi.\nPositive real arguments have zero phase in this principal specialization.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(z^n)=n\\,\\mathrm{Ln}z,\\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z.\nThe integer exponent is represented by the real integer-power operation.\n\n\\ln(z^n)=n\\,\\ln z,\\quad n\\in\\mathbb Z,\\quad-\\pi\\leq n\\operatorname{ph}z\\leq\\pi.\nFor a positive real base the phase condition is automatic.\n\n\\ln(1/z)=-\\ln z,\\quad|\\operatorname{ph}z|\\leq\\pi.\nFor positive real arguments the principal phase condition is automatic.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(\\exp z)=z+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad k\\in\\mathbb Z.\nThe existential integer records the branch ambiguity of the complex logarithm.\n\n\\ln(\\exp z)=z,\\quad-\\pi\\leq\\Im z\\leq\\pi.\nMathlib's principal branch uses a strict lower boundary in the formal hypothesis.\n\n\\exp(\\ln z)=\\exp(\\mathrm{Ln}z)=z.\nFor every nonzero complex argument, exponentiating its principal logarithm recovers it.\n\n\\mathrm{Ln}(a^z)=z\\,\\mathrm{Ln}a+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad a\\ne0.\nFor positive real base and real exponent there is no branch ambiguity.\n\n\\ln(a^z)=z\\,\\ln a+2k\\pi\\mathrm{i},\\quad a\\ne0.\nThe positive-real principal specialization removes the branch integer.\n\n\\ln(a^x)=x\\ln a,\\quad a>0.\nThis is the source's real-variable identity in its natural positive-base form.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Elementary Functions\u0009§4.8 Identities\u0009§4.8 Identities","header":"§4.8(i) Logarithms","id":"/Elementary-Functions/___4___8-Identities/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Elementary-Functions--___4___8-Identities--___4___8-Identities--___4___8_LPAR_i_RPAR_-Logarithms"},"/Gamma-Function/___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions--___5___11_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Ratios":{"contents":"In this subsection the source allows real or complex constants and approaches\nin a sector. The declarations below give positive-real specializations, so\nall powers and Gamma values use their unambiguous real meanings.\n\nThe generalized Bernoulli polynomials are canonical formal coefficients. Their\nformal generating series raises the Bernoulli series by the binomial map and\nmultiplies by the formal exponential; the analytic generating-function law is\nthen stated separately. The ratio coefficients below are defined from this\ncanonical map.\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim z^{a-b}.\n\n\n\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim z^{a-b}\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{G_k(a,b)}{z^k}.\n\n\n\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)}{\\Gamma(z+b)}\\sim\n\\left(z+\\frac{a+b-1}{2}\\right)^{a-b}\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}\\frac{H_k(a,b)}{(z+(a+b-1)/2)^{2k}}.\n\n\n\nG_0=1,\\quad G_1=\\frac12(a-b)(a+b-1),\\quad\nG_2=\\frac1{12}{a-b\\choose2}\\left(3(a+b-1)^2-(a-b+1)\\right).\nThe generalized binomial coefficient is the finite falling product\ngeneralizedBinomial introduced in the shared vocabulary block above.\n\n\nH_0=1,\\quad H_1=-\\frac1{12}{a-b\\choose2}(a-b+1),\\quad\nH_2=\\frac1{240}{a-b\\choose4}\\left(2(a-b+1)+5(a-b+1)^2\\right).\n\n\n\nG_k(a,b)={a-b\\choose k}B_k^{(a-b+1)}(a).\n\n\n\nH_k(a,b)={a-b\\choose 2k}B_{2k}^{(a-b+1)}\\left(\\frac{a-b+1}{2}\\right).\n\n\n\n\\frac{\\Gamma(z+a)\\Gamma(z+b)}{\\Gamma(z+c)}\\sim\n\\sum_{k=0}^{\\infty}(-1)^k\\frac{(c-a)_k(c-b)_k}{k!}\n\\Gamma(a+b-c+z-k).\nOn positive real z, the summand is exposed as gammaProductTerm, using the\nsame finite rising factorial as 5.11.5.\n\n","context":"LMLF · DLMF with Lean\u0009Gamma Function\u0009§5.11 Asymptotic Expansions","header":"§5.11(iii) Ratios","id":"/Gamma-Function/___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions/#LMLF-___-DLMF-with-Lean--Gamma-Function--___5___11-Asymptotic-Expansions--___5___11_LPAR_iii_RPAR_-Ratios"}});